// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-017
//
// CASE 5, SINCE CYCLE 0.1.3c: THE CALL IS REFUSED (TM-194). `vec_at` takes
// `T: Pod`, a trait only a type that owns nothing can implement as it is
// declared, so at `T = string` each call below is `NITPICK-TYPE-017` --
// "`string` does not implement `Pod`, which `vec_at`'s parameter `T`
// requires" -- and the move this file measured cannot be written any more.
// THE REFUSAL IS THE ASSERTION (B-7). It is the library's refusal, not the
// pin's: the same text is refused the same way at `c3bdae2`.
//
// ITS CONTROLS, in `TRANSCRIPT.txt`'s last section at both pins and on both
// legs: the file as it stood, against the library as it stood -- `exit 11`,
// the destructive read -- and this text against a `vec_at` that takes its
// `Vec` as a loan but has no bound, which exits 11 as well: the loan does not
// close it, the bound does. The two calls read `v` and no longer `@v`,
// because `vec_at` reads a loan since the same cycle; written `@v` they add
// `NITPICK-TYPE-007` to the refusal, a pointer where a `Vec` is lent.
//
// EVERYTHING BELOW, TO THE `mod:` LINE, IS THE RECORD AS CYCLE 0.0.5 WROTE IT,
// under the marker it carried then, `expect-exit: 11`. Its last paragraph's
// "if a later pin makes `vec_at` a non-destructive read, this file goes red"
// came true another way: the library made the destructive read unwritable.
//
// CASE 5 -- THE LIBRARY'S OWN `vec_at<T>`, AND THIS ONE IS NOT A COMPILER
// DEFECT. It is the language behaving as specified, producing a result the
// library's own header claimed was impossible.
//
// `src/core/vec.npk`'s `vec_at<T>` ends `pass s[i];`. `pass` of a PLACE at an
// owning type MOVES IMPLICITLY -- specified, and the compiler's own note on
// the 1.2.2 sweep counts the 146 sites that stopped needing an explicit
// `move` when it did. So at `T = string` the emitted body loads the element,
// stores `zeroinitializer` over the slot and calls `npk.vacant.<dty>` on it:
// the element is REMOVED from the `Vec` by a function called `at`, and
// `count` is not touched, so the container still says it holds one.
//
// THE MARKER IS ASSERTED, unlike case1's and case4's, and the difference is
// the point: this behaviour is CORRECT and will not change when the TYPE-046
// gap closes, so committing it as an expectation commits a true statement
// about the language rather than a bug. If a later pin makes `vec_at` a
// non-destructive read, this file goes red and somebody reads why.
//
//   exit 11 = the second read saw a length-0 string -> `vec_at` is DESTRUCTIVE
//   exit 0  = both reads saw 11 bytes               -> `vec_at` is a read
//   exit 20 = the FIRST read was already wrong      -> the probe is wrong
//   exit 21 = `count` moved                         -> the probe is wrong
//
// WHAT IT CHANGES. `src/core/vec.npk`'s header said, of `vec_at`, "at an
// owning `T` a copy is refused by TYPE-046". That is false in both halves:
// nothing refuses it, and what happens instead is not a copy. Corrected at
// 0.0.5 by TM-136, in the same commit as this file.
mod:case5_vec_at_destructive;

use "../../../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    Vec<string>:v = raw vec_init::<string>(2i64);
    drop vec_push(@v, string_concat("Europe/", "Kyiv"));   // 11 bytes

    string:a = raw vec_at::<string>(v, 0i64);
    int64:na = string_byte_length(a);
    if (na != 11i64) { exit 20i32; }

    // `vec_at` does not touch `count`, so the container still claims one
    // element. Read the same index again.
    if (v.count != 1i64) { exit 21i32; }
    string:b = raw vec_at::<string>(v, 0i64);
    int64:nb = string_byte_length(b);

    drop vec_free(@v);
    if (nb == 0i64)  { exit 11i32; }
    if (nb == 11i64) { exit 0i32; }
    exit 12i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (StackExhausted) { exit 106i32; },
        (MachineFault)   { exit 107i32; },
        (LimitViolated)  { exit 109i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
